Take A = {n} and α(n) = 1. The condition gives ‖x_n‖ = 1.

**Step 2: Upper bound via triangle inequality.**

For any finitely supported sequence a = (a_1, …, a_N),

$$\left\|\sum_{n=1}^N a_n x_n\right\| \le \sum_{n=1}^N |a_n|\,\|x_n\| = \sum_{n=1}^N |a_n| = \|a\|_1.$$

**Step 3: Key lemma — every sign pattern is realized by a norm-one functional.**

Let A = {n_1, …, n_k} be finite and let α: A → {−1, 1}. By hypothesis,

$$\left\|\sum_{n \in A} \alpha_n x_n\right\| = |A| = k.$$

By the Hahn–Banach theorem, there exists f ∈ X* with ‖f‖ = 1 such that

$$f\!\left(\sum_{n \in A} \alpha_n x_n\right) = k, \quad\text{i.e.,}\quad \sum_{n \in A} \alpha_n\, f(x_n) = k.$$

Since |f(x_n)| ≤ ‖f‖·‖x_n‖ = 1 for each n, each term α_n f(x_n) satisfies |α_n f(x_n)| ≤ 1. The sum of k terms, each at most 1 in absolute value, equals k. This forces

$$\alpha_n\, f(x_n) = 1 \quad\text{for all } n \in A, \quad\text{i.e.,}\quad f(x_n) = \alpha_n.$$

So: **for every finite A and every sign function α on A, there exists a norm-one functional f ∈ X* with f(x_n) = α(n) for all n ∈ A.**

